(TIM-21 - a timer tracks all three of the things the manual says it does:
 a start time, an end time, and the duration between them. The job here is
 a known one second, so all three can be checked against each other rather
 than printed.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-21.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   the start time is a unix stamp from this run
   the end time is not before the start time
   the duration is the second that was waited
)
Get current time into 'the reference clock'.
a timer called 'job timer'.
Start the 'job timer'.
Wait 1 second.
Stop the 'job timer'.
a number called 'the start stamp' is the 'job timer''s start time.
a number called 'the end stamp' is the 'job timer''s end time.
a number called 'the measured job' is the 'job timer''s duration in milliseconds.
If 'the start stamp' is greater than or equal to 'the reference clock''s unix then, print "the start time is a unix stamp from this run".
Otherwise, print "the start time {'the start stamp'} predates the program".
If 'the end stamp' is greater than or equal to 'the start stamp' then, print "the end time is not before the start time".
Otherwise, print "the end time {'the end stamp'} is before the start time {'the start stamp'}".
If 'the measured job' is greater than 999 and 'the measured job' is less than 3000 then, print "the duration is the second that was waited".
Otherwise, print "the duration is {'the measured job'} milliseconds".
